Disclosed licence activation: on boot and once a day this software sends a
licence-compliance ping to LICENSE_SERVER_URL, with or without a LICENSE_KEY:
the licence token when one is set, a random per-install id, the POS API
version, the origin the copy is served from, whether it is a development run
and, only from a copy without a valid key, how many client launches it has
counted — no sales, catalogue or personal data, and nothing about who used the
copy. The receiving server also keeps the network address the ping came from.
It is best-effort and never disables the software — a licence-compliance check,
not a Closed System, consistent with the LICENSE. See README.md and
TechDocs/POS_LICENSING.md.

Disclosed notice: a copy without a valid LICENSE_KEY counts client launches in
its own database and, past a threshold (30 by default), shows every user of the
POS a strip of text saying that it runs without a licence key. The licence
server's answer to the ping may set that threshold, the text, and whether the
same line is shown to the guests of the copy's QR menu — settings only. The
notice is text: it never blocks, limits or slows the software, and nothing the
licence server answers can switch a copy off. A copy with a valid key counts
nothing and shows no notice.

Disclosed update check: when the user presses "install update", the official
desktop cashier adds an X-Delivery-Token header (a licence fingerprint, a scope
and an expiry, signed; not the licence key; no user activity or personal data)
to its request for the update manifest at the-live.shop. It can only make the
author's update server refuse an update, never disable a copy that is running.

